From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 22:23:10 +0200
Subject: mtd: rawnand: lantiq-xrx500: wait on R/B# alone, and observe tWB

Programs were silently dropping pages. One page in roughly five thousand
came back pristine-erased with valid ECC, its neighbours intact, and
every layer reporting success -- no MTD error, no ECC event, nothing in
dmesg.

The chip-ready wait was concluding on EBU_WAIT_WR_C. The bit belongs to
the byte handshake, and it is still set from the PAGEPROG opcode strobed
a moment earlier, so the wait returns immediately, inside tPROG.
nand_prog_page_end_op() then reads status, which only fails on
NAND_STATUS_FAIL, and passes. The next page's entire command / address /
data / PAGEPROG sequence is issued to a die that is still busy, and the
die discards it. What survives is one erased page and rc = 0.

The reason it is rare rather than constant is that the byte handshake's
own poll usually consumes WR_C on the way past. Removing WR_C from this
predicate and instrumenting the one it would have exited on shows the
shipped form mis-exiting on 149490 of 149504 page programs -- so the
handshake's incidental consumption was doing all of the work of keeping
the window shut. Measured with the shipped predicate: 29 dropped pages
in 149504 page programs, or 1.9e-4 per program. With R/B# alone: none in
30720, while the same counter shows the mechanism arming eleven times
and being caught eleven times.

Widening the EBU timing makes it worse rather than better (CMULT 1 -> 2
took it to 0.6 drops per pass), which is consistent with WR_C being set
later and escaping consumption more often, and is the most likely reason
a GRX350 board shows nothing across nineteen thousand programs while a
GRX550 board reproduces it inside a day.

Observing instr->delay_ns is the other half. The core hangs tWB on the
PAGEPROG and ERASE2 command instructions and the WAITRDY behind them,
this driver never waited it, and so R/B# was being sampled before the
die had had a chance to pull it low. That the read path never showed a
symptom is not luck -- a read concluding early re-reads through the same
data path and self-heals -- but the write path has nothing to fall back
on.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/lantiq-xrx500-nand.c b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/lantiq-xrx500-nand.c
index b7f2e1cc4df0..7fef22970f3c 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/lantiq-xrx500-nand.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/lantiq-xrx500-nand.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/bitops.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/err.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
@@ -144,16 +145,18 @@ static int xrx500_nand_wait_latch(struct nand_chip *chip)
 				  20, XRX500_LATCH_TIMEOUT_US);
 }
 
-/* And this one is the chip-ready wait, for the WAITRDY instruction. */
+/*
+ * And this one is the chip-ready wait, for the WAITRDY instruction. R/B# is
+ * the only flag that answers it: WR_C belongs to the byte handshake above,
+ * and accepting it here means returning while the die is still busy.
+ */
 static int xrx500_nand_waitrdy(struct nand_chip *chip, u32 timeout_us)
 {
 	struct xrx500_nand_host *host = nand_to_xrx500(chip);
 	u32 status;
 
 	return readl_poll_timeout(host->ebu + EBU_WAIT, status,
-				  (status & EBU_WAIT_RDBY) ||
-				  (status & EBU_WAIT_WR_C),
-				  20, timeout_us);
+				  status & EBU_WAIT_RDBY, 20, timeout_us);
 }
 
 static int xrx500_nand_writeb(struct nand_chip *chip, u32 offset, u8 value)
@@ -295,6 +298,17 @@ static int xrx500_nand_exec_op(struct nand_chip *chip,
 
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
+
+		/*
+		 * Observe the instruction's own delay before moving on. This
+		 * is what makes R/B# meaningful for the wait that follows a
+		 * program or an erase: the core hangs tWB on the PAGEPROG /
+		 * ERASE2 command instruction and the WAITRDY behind it, so
+		 * skipping the delay samples R/B# before the die has had time
+		 * to pull it low.
+		 */
+		if (instr->delay_ns)
+			ndelay(instr->delay_ns);
 	}
 
 	return 0;
